{"name":"Bedazzled","hex":"#d3b9cc","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"bedazzled","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Covered in spangles, glitter, jewels, or similar shiny decorations.","Experiencing dazzlement; overcome by awe and appreciation."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of bedazzle"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bedazzled"}],"entries":[{"title":"Bedazzled (1967 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Bedazzled is a 1967 British DeLuxe Color fantasy comedy film produced and directed by Stanley Donen in Panavision format. It was written by comedian Peter Cook and stars both Cook and his comedy partner Dudley Moore. It is a comic retelling of the Faust legend, set in the Swinging London of the 1960s. In the film, the Devil (Cook) offers an unhappy young man (Moore) seven wishes in return for his soul, but twists the spirit of the wishes to frustrate the man's hopes."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/70/Bedazzled_Original_UK_cinema_release_poster.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bedazzled_(1967_film)","wikidata":"Q934251"},{"title":"Bedazzled (2000 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Bedazzled is a 2000 fantasy romantic comedy film directed by Harold Ramis and starring Brendan Fraser and Elizabeth Hurley. It is a remake of the 1967 British film of the same name, written by Peter Cook and Dudley Moore, itself a comic retelling of the Faust legend. It follows a computer company worker (Fraser) who makes a deal with the Devil (Hurley) to receive seven wishes in exchange for his soul so he can pursue the woman he loves, though the Devil twists the spirit of each wish to frustrate him."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/6/6a/Bedazzled.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bedazzled_(2000_film)","w